# ApplyOnce — local configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in, then export the variables in your shell:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#     python3 serve.py
#
# ApplyOnce does not read `.env` automatically — there is no dotenv in the
# standard library, and pulling one in would break the "standard library only"
# promise that every other module here keeps. You are in charge of loading it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apify (optional) — only needed for verified email lookup
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Get yours at https://console.apify.com/settings/integrations
# create an Actor API token, then:
#
#   export APIFY_TOKEN=apify_api_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# The browser also accepts a token typed into the UI, which is stored in that
# browser's localStorage and sent directly to api.apify.com. Never write a token
# into a tracked file, and rotate anything pasted into a chat or a commit.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP (optional) — needed only by mailer.py
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials are read from the environment at send time only. They are never
# written to the SQLite database, never sent to the browser, and never accepted
# as a command-line flag. mailer.py deliberately has no --password option.

# Gmail / Outlook 365 / Fastmail / Proton Bridge:
#   587 with STARTTLS, or 465 with implicit TLS.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USERNAME=

# An app password, NOT your account password. Never a real password.
SMTP_APP_PASSWORD=

# The From identity recipients will see. CAN-SPAM requires a truthful sender
# name and a valid physical mailing address in every commercial message;
# mailer.py refuses to send without both.
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_FROM_NAME=

# Your real mailing address. Required. mailer.py appends it to every message.
SMTP_PHYSICAL_ADDRESS=

# Where an unsubscribe should point.
#
# With an https base, mailer.py adds both List-Unsubscribe and
# List-Unsubscribe-Post: One-Click, so Gmail can honour an unsubscribe without
# the recipient doing anything. That needs an endpoint YOU host that can write
# to this database — the Vercel function cannot, because its filesystem is
# read-only.
#
# Leave it unset and mailer.py falls back to a mailto: that asks the recipient
# to reply. Weaker, but it needs nothing hosted.
# SMTP_UNSUBSCRIBE_URL_BASE=https://your-app.example
SMTP_UNSUBSCRIBE_MAILBOX=

# Ceilings. Both exist to protect your sending reputation, and both are
# enforced per run rather than trusted to you.
SMTP_DAILY_LIMIT=50
SMTP_DELAY_SECONDS=30
SMTP_RETRIES=2
SMTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the SQLite file lives. Defaults to ./applyonce.db.
# APPLYONCE_DB=data/research.db